.TH TANGSIBLE-ROLE 1 "August 2026" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.SH NAME
tangsible-role \- run a single role in isolation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tangsible role
.I role_name
.RI [ "ansible\-playbook args" ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Testing a single role normally means hand\-writing a temporary playbook
around it first.
.B role
does that for you: it generates a minimal stub playbook
(
.B hosts: all
,
.B roles: [
.I role_name
.B ]
) in the current directory, runs it exactly as
.BR tangsible-run (1)
would, and removes the stub again once Tangsible exits. The stub is
written to
.BI .tangsible\-role\- role_name .yml
(dot\-prefixed, so it doesn't clutter a directory listing); it should be
added to
.BR .gitignore ,
which Tangsible does not do automatically. If Tangsible is killed
before it can clean up (a crash, a
.BR "kill \-9" ),
a stale copy can be left behind \(em safe to delete by hand.
.PP
The stub always targets
.BR "hosts: all" ;
narrow the actual target the normal way, with
.B \-i
/
.B \-l
passthrough arguments. If a
.B ./roles/\fIrole_name\fR
directory isn't found relative to the current directory, a warning is
printed up front \(em the run proceeds regardless (Ansible may still
resolve the role from
.BR $ANSIBLE_ROLES_PATH ,
a configured roles path, or a collection), but the drill\-down view's
Task definition section won't be available for it, since the source
lookup only ever walks the stub's own directory tree.
.PP
Everything else \(em the live tree, filtering, the rerun dialog, the
diff picker \(em works exactly as in
.BR tangsible-run (1).
A rerun started from this session regenerates a fresh stub for the same
role each time; it is never reused across separate
.B tangsible role
invocations.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Same as
.BR tangsible-run (1);
see
.BR tangsible (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tangsible (1),
.BR tangsible-run (1),
.BR tangsible-rerun (1)
